64-bit integers and 80-bit values copied through the FPU keep all their bits: FILD m64 and FLD m80 put the exact
80 bits in rawst (the register's raw value, as FRSTOR does) instead of converting them to a double, which has 53
bits of mantissa; FLD ST(i) copies them, and FISTP m64 and FSTP m80 store them as they are while no instruction has
written the register since. Programs that move memory 8 bytes at a time with FILD/FISTP (a common way on CPUs
without MMX or SSE) had bytes changed: a value beyond 2^53 lost its low bits.

